# The PLATFORM's own pages are indexable (owner-ruled: "fuck the no-index
# lockin — trove.usecontextlayer.com is indexed"). The standard's unconditional
# noindex (§5) binds ARTIFACTS, and each artifact's generated _headers carries
# it; the platform is an artifact in spirit, not in exactness. The registry's
# Worker routes (/a/*, /register) keep noindex in code — they are
# artifact-adjacent surfaces, and _headers never reaches Worker responses.

# charset=utf-8 on the text types this directory serves. Not a standard
# requirement — §5 mandates only noindex, and a trove may serve text in any
# encoding. It is here because the host appends no charset of its own, so
# /AGENTS.md went out bare: markdown has no in-band mechanism the way HTML has
# <meta charset>, and every reader decoding it as CP1252 saw each em dash as
# "â€”". (`wrangler dev` DOES add one locally, which is how the opposite was
# once written down as measured. Trust production.)
#
# These are UNCONDITIONAL, unlike an artifact's generated _headers, which
# declares a charset only where the bytes are UTF-8. That is safe only because
# every file under public/ is UTF-8 today and the platform-manifest test pins
# their bytes. Adding a non-UTF-8 file here would have it mislabelled by these
# rules and nothing would catch it — convert it, or narrow the rule.
#
# /trove.json is omitted deliberately: application/json defines no charset
# parameter and is UTF-8 by definition.
/
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/*.js
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
